  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1213 PM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nIsolated severe thunderstorms are possible this afternoon, mainly\nalong and south of a cold front in north Mississippi and east-\ncentral Arkansas. A very moist and strongly unstable air mass is\nin place across the region, featuring PW values over 2 inches and\nSBCAPE over 3,000 J/kg. CIN is weakening as temperatures warm,\nand most of the area should reach convective temperatures around\n97 degrees by 1 PM. DCAPE values from 1,200-1,400 J/kg will make\nstrong downdrafts very likely this afternoon and evening, with\ndamaging winds being the primary threat.\n\nThe aforementioned cold front is currently moving southwestward\nacross the Mid-South. This abnormal motion is the result of high\npressure over the Great Lakes and a deep trough along the East\nCoast, resulting in northerly flow across the Ohio and Middle to\nLower Mississippi River valleys. The air mass behind the front is\nhighly modified but continental in nature, so it will not result\nin much of a temperature change as it passes. However, a wind\nshift and slightly lower relative humidity may be noticeable\nacross much of the region late this afternoon and especially\ntomorrow. The lower dew points and relative humidity should keep\nheat indices below advisory criteria (above 105 degrees) on\nMonday despite afternoon highs in the mid-to-upper 90s across\nmost of the area. The coolest high temperatures tomorrow are\nexpected near the Tennessee River, where areas around Camden and\nParis should top out near 91 degrees.\n\nA ridge currently over the Plains will rapidly expand across the\ncentral and lower Mississippi River Valley late tomorrow into\nTuesday. As a result, temperatures will rebound. Additionally,\nsoutheasterly winds will return to the region, leading to\nincreasing relative humidity. Another round of Heat Advisories\nwill likely be needed, initially in the Delta portions of east-\ncentral Arkansas and northwest Mississippi on Tuesday, expanding\nacross much of the region on Wednesday.\n\nThe highest chance of widespread rainfall seen in quite a while\nis expected to return to the Mid-South on Thursday. A ridge\nacross the southern Plains and southeastern U.S. will flatten out\nlate Tuesday through Wednesday. Guidance is in fairly good\nagreement that a cold front associated with a deep low tracking\nacross eastern Canada will approach the Mid-South on Thursday.\nDetails regarding rainfall amounts and potential for strong to\nsevere storms will come into better focus over the next couple of\ndays, but overall expect slightly cooler temperatures and a fair\nchance of precipitation Thursday and Friday.",
